seg: opencv-python-headless and pinned build backends. Apply from the repository
root with deploy/patches/apply-pyproject-patches.sh (git apply, then `uv lock`; only the opencv entries
and a [manifest] table change in uv.lock).

opencv: opencv-python's cv2 needs libGL.so.1, libglib2.0-0, libgthread, libX11 and libxcb at import
time (checked with ldd on the locked wheel); the headless wheel of the same version needs only libc, libm,
libgcc_s and libstdc++ (checked the same way). With it the runtime image has no apt packages at all,
no X11 or mesa libraries to patch, and the wheel is 13 MB smaller. The service never opens a window.
Both wheels provide the module cv2, so installing both would clash: the direct dependency becomes the
headless wheel at the locked version (4.11.0.86) and an override drops every other package's
opencv-python requirement with a marker that never matches (the trick services/cls uses for ipyplot).
Without this patch, build the image with --build-arg OPENCV_GUI_LIBS=1 instead.

build pins: the git dependencies are built from source during `uv sync`, in an isolated build
environment whose backend uv resolves fresh from PyPI. That code runs at install time with network
access, so an unpinned backend is a supply-chain hole and makes the image non-reproducible, although
uv.lock pins every runtime package. [tool.uv] build-constraint-dependencies pins the backend and its
own dependencies (and uv-build, the projects' own backend, to the version CI uses). Tried: the
constraints are honored when the git dependency is built (uv -v shows setuptools>=84.0.0,<84.0.0+).
Prebuilding and hashing wheels was the alternative; it would take the git dependencies out of
uv.lock, which is worse. uv.lock records the constraints in its [manifest], so `uv sync --locked`
needs the lock refreshed after the patch: apply-pyproject-patches.sh does it.

--- a/services/seg/pyproject.toml
+++ b/services/seg/pyproject.toml
@@ -11,7 +11,7 @@
     "decimer-segmentation",
     "decimer-service",
     "numpy==1.26.3",
-    "opencv-python>=4.11.0.86",
+    "opencv-python-headless==4.11.0.86",
     "protobuf==4.25.1",
     "tensorflow==2.15.1",
 ]
@@ -47,6 +47,25 @@
     "slow: runs the real Mask R-CNN on rendered pages of the golden PDF (CPU, about 35 s); select with -m slow",
 ]
 
+[tool.uv]
+# The service imports cv2 but never opens a window. opencv-python also needs libGL.so.1 and
+# libglib2.0-0 at import time; the headless wheel does not, so the runtime image needs no apt
+# packages. decimer-segmentation declares opencv-python, so it is dropped with a marker that
+# never matches (the same trick as ipyplot in services/cls), and the headless wheel, pinned to
+# the version that was locked before (the two wheels share a version scheme), replaces it.
+override-dependencies = [
+    "opencv-python ; sys_platform == 'never'",
+]
+# Build backends are pinned too (deploy/patches). decimer-segmentation is built from source at
+# install time (setup.py, setuptools), in an isolated environment. Without this uv takes
+# the newest setuptools and wheel on PyPI that day and runs it. Versions: the newest releases
+# that were two weeks old on 2026-10-22. uv-build is the project's own backend (CI uses 0.13.5).
+build-constraint-dependencies = [
+    "setuptools==84.0.0",
+    "wheel==0.48.0",
+    "uv-build==0.13.5",
+]
+
 [tool.uv.sources]
 decimer-segmentation = { git = "https://github.com/Kohulan/DECIMER-Image-Segmentation", rev = "6914cacea266d59e4dcbdae549b0ffff39fcc604" }
 decimer-service = { path = "../common", editable = true }
